Mitcad's patch to OCCT 8.0.1, under OCCT's licence:
SPDX-License-Identifier: LGPL-2.1-only WITH OCCT-exception-1.0

Collections left intact when an allocation in them throws: with the previous
patches an allocation that fails throws instead of crashing, and the
algorithms that catch it go on and later destroy their collections. Some
collections were left with a freed block or rewired nodes:
- NCollection_Array1::Resize without its data freed the old block before
  allocating the new one, and kept pointing to it when the allocation
  threw (freed again by its destructor); NCollection_LocalArray likewise.
  They are empty while the new block is allocated.
- NCollection_IndexedMap and NCollection_IndexedDataMap::ReSize moved
  their nodes to the new buckets and only then reallocated their index
  array: when that threw, the old buckets still held nodes linked into the
  new chains, and the map's destructor freed nodes twice (a "double free"
  that ended the process in a boolean, BOPAlgo_WireSplitter, under a memory
  limit). The index array is reallocated first.
- NCollection_BaseMap::BeginResize freed nothing when the second bucket
  array failed (a leak).
diff --git a/src/FoundationClasses/TKernel/NCollection/NCollection_Array1.hxx b/src/FoundationClasses/TKernel/NCollection/NCollection_Array1.hxx
index 36107c7..ef40145 100644
--- a/src/FoundationClasses/TKernel/NCollection/NCollection_Array1.hxx
+++ b/src/FoundationClasses/TKernel/NCollection/NCollection_Array1.hxx
@@ -482,6 +482,9 @@ protected:
       const size_t aMinSize = (std::min)(theNewSize, mySize);
       if (myIsOwner)
       {
+        // The elements past a smaller size are destroyed: a reallocation
+        // that throws leaves the array at that size.
+        mySize    = (std::min)(mySize, theNewSize);
         myPointer = myAllocator.reallocate(myPointer, theNewSize);
       }
       else
@@ -497,6 +500,11 @@ protected:
       {
         myAllocator.deallocate(aPrevPtr, mySize);
       }
+      // Empty while the new block is allocated: an allocation that throws
+      // leaves no freed block to free again.
+      myPointer = nullptr;
+      mySize    = 0;
+      myIsOwner = false;
       myPointer = myAllocator.allocate(theNewSize);
       construct(0, theNewSize);
     }
